{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794745044,"nonce":"2yP2GeJWyJ6aqFnCtY6aGNrmna4=","message":"Specialization and the other things the bytecode never carried (design, before body lowering).\n\nWhy this is the point of nAIR: the HashLink generator erases generics. A type parameter becomes its constraint class or Dynamic, so a `Box\u003cInt\u003e` stores its field boxed, a call argument of type T that is a number is passed as Null\u003cnumber\u003e, the result of a call returning T is cast back, and every `T` operation in the body goes through those casts and null checks. The only container the generator specializes is Array (element type picks ArrayBytes_Int, ArrayBytes_Float, ArrayObj and the others). `@:generic` copies a class or function per instantiation, but only where the author wrote it; the std does not.\n\nThe TAST still has the instantiations: a class type carries its arguments, a `new` node and a call's callee carry the instantiated type, and format 4 (this commit series) lists the type parameters each class, method, enum and abstract declares, so substitution is possible.\n\nOrder of work:\n1. Function and method monomorphization, no layout change. Collect the instantiations reachable from main; each (function, type arguments) gets a copy lowered with T replaced. Inside a copy for T = Int, arithmetic is native and there are no Dynamic casts or null checks; fields of type T are still stored boxed. Sound, because objects keep their layout.\n2. Constraint devirtualization: in a copy for T = Circle, `t.area()` has a known receiver and can be called directly or inlined.\n3. Class layout specialization: `Box\u003cInt\u003e` gets an i32 field. This changes the object, so it is allowed only where the closed world shows the value never reaches a position typed with the erased class, Dynamic, Reflect, Type or Serializer, or native code; otherwise the generic layout stays. Identity is the risk: Haxe lets `Box\u003cInt\u003e` and `Box\u003cDynamic\u003e` flow into each other.\n4. Structural shapes: an anonymous structure whose concrete shape is known at its construction and uses gets fixed field offsets instead of a hashed lookup through a virtual type.\n\nLimits to design in from the start: code growth (specialize value-type arguments first; reference-type arguments share a copy unless a constraint devirtualizes), polymorphic recursion (a function calling itself at a larger type, such as f\u003cT\u003e calling f\u003cArray\u003cT\u003e\u003e, needs a depth cap and falls back to the generic copy), and stack-trace and reflection names for the copies.\n\nOther facts the bytecode lacks, each its own pass with its own measurement: closed-world devirtualization of final and never-overridden receivers, constructor scalar replacement without inlining the constructor, Null\u003cT\u003e and null-check elimination, array element types and bounds facts, which tries can throw, enum switch exhaustiveness, immutability of final and inline variables, closure inlining.\n\nFirst targets on the rows we trail: knucleotide (Map\u003cString, Int\u003e boxes every value) and anything with List\u003cFloat\u003e or Array\u003cArray\u003cFloat\u003e\u003e. spectralnorm and fasta have no generics; they wait for the devirtualization and bounds passes.","files":null}]}